╭─ VULN-0001 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Path Traversal in /file route                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/scan-canary                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /file                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A path traversal vulnerability in the `/file` route of the Flask            │
│  application allows unauthenticated attackers to read arbitrary files from   │
│  the server filesystem via the `name` parameter.                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Arbitrary file read (C:H, I:N, A:N) enabling exposure of source code,       │
│  configuration files, and sensitive system files.                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The `/file` handler reads the `name` parameter directly from query          │
│  arguments and passes it to `os.path.join("/var/data", ...)` without         │
│  checks. In Python, `os.path.join` appends strings, and if an absolute path  │
│  is provided as a subsequent argument, it discards everything before it.     │
│  Additionally, standard `../` segments allow escaping the `/var/data`        │
│  directory.                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Send a GET request to `/file?name=../../etc/passwd`.                     │
│  2. Observe the contents of `/etc/passwd` returned in the response.          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import requests                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  res = requests.get("http://localhost:5000/file", params={"name":            │
│  "../../etc/passwd"})                                                        │
│  print(res.text)                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:19-21                                                  │
│    Fix path traversal by validating the resolved path stays within           │
│  /var/data                                                                   │
│    @app.route("/file")                                                       │
│  def read():                                                                 │
│      return open(os.path.join("/var/data", request.args.get("name",          │
│  ""))).read()                                                                │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    - @app.route("/file")                                                     │
│  def read():                                                                 │
│      return open(os.path.join("/var/data", request.args.get("name",          │
│  ""))).read()                                                                │
│    + @app.route("/file")                                                     │
│  def read():                                                                 │
│      name = request.args.get("name", "")                                     │
│      safe_path = os.path.abspath(os.path.join("/var/data", name))            │
│      if not safe_path.startswith("/var/data"):                               │
│          return "Access Denied", 403                                         │
│      return open(safe_path).read()                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  1. Resolve the requested path using `os.path.abspath` or                    │
│  `os.path.realpath`.                                                         │
│  2. Ensure the resulting path starts with the intended base directory        │
│  (`/var/data`).                                                              │
│  3. Return an error (e.g., 403 Forbidden) if the path validation fails.      │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0002 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Command Injection in /run Route                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: CRITICAL                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 9.8                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/scan-canary                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /run                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The /run endpoint executes arbitrary shell commands supplied via the cmd    │
│  query parameter, enabling remote code execution.                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Remote code execution as the running user on the server.                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The handler retrieves the cmd parameter from the query string and passes    │
│  it directly to `subprocess.check_output([\"/bin/sh\", \"-c\", cmd])`,       │
│  allowing command injection via shell metacharacters.                        │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Send a request to /run with a custom cmd parameter.\n2. The server       │
│  executes the command and returns output.                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import requests\nres = requests.get('http://127.0.0.1:5000/run',            │
│  params={'cmd': 'id'})\nprint(res.text)                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:13-16                                                  │
│    Sanitize command execution                                                │
│        cmd = request.args.get(\"cmd\", \"id\")\n    return                   │
│  subprocess.check_output([\"/bin/sh\", \"-c\", cmd]).decode()                │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     cmd = request.args.get(\"cmd\", \"id\")\n    return                 │
│  subprocess.check_output([\"/bin/sh\", \"-c\", cmd]).decode()                │
│    +     import shlex\n    cmd = request.args.get(\"cmd\", \"id\")\n         │
│  safe_cmd = shlex.quote(cmd)\n    return                                     │
│  subprocess.check_output([\"/bin/sh\", \"-c\", safe_cmd]).decode()           │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Sanitize user input or avoid invoking a shell via subprocess with           │
│  attacker-controlled data.                                                   │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0003 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: SQL Injection in /user endpoint via id parameter                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 8.2                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/scan-canary                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:L/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The /user endpoint constructs an SQL query using string formatting with     │
│  user-supplied input, allowing attackers to inject arbitrary SQL commands    │
│  and access or manipulate the database.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Attackers can read, modify, or delete database records, potentially         │
│  leading to unauthorized access to sensitive data, authentication bypass,    │
│  or complete database compromise.                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The vulnerability exists in the user() function at line 10 of app.py. The   │
│  uid parameter is obtained directly from request.args.get("id", "1") and     │
│  interpolated into the SQL query string using the % operator. This allows    │
│  an attacker to control the SQL query executed against the database.         │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Start the Flask application.                                             │
│  2. Send a GET request to /user?id=1 OR 1=1.                                 │
│  3. Observe that the response contains all rows from the users table         │
│  instead of just the row with id=1.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  import sqlite3                                                              │
│  import tempfile                                                             │
│  import sys                                                                  │
│  sys.path.append('/workspace/scan-canary')                                   │
│  from app import app                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  # Create a temporary database with test data                                │
│  temp_db = tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(delete=False)                         │
│  temp_db.close()                                                             │
│  conn = sqlite3.connect(temp_db.name)                                        │
│  conn.execute("CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, username TEXT,    │
│  secret TEXT)")                                                              │
│  conn.execute("INSERT INTO users (username, secret) VALUES ('admin',         │
│  'ADMIN_SECRET_123')")                                                       │
│  conn.execute("INSERT INTO users (username, secret) VALUES ('user1',         │
│  'USER1_SECRET_456')")                                                       │
│  conn.commit()                                                               │
│  conn.close()                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  # Monkey-patch sqlite3.connect to use our temporary database                │
│  original_connect = sqlite3.connect                                          │
│  def mock_connect(*args, **kwargs):                                          │
│      return original_connect(temp_db.name)                                   │
│  sqlite3.connect = mock_connect                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  # Test the vulnerability                                                    │
│  client = app.test_client()                                                  │
│  print("Testing normal request (id=1):")                                     │
│  resp1 = client.get('/user?id=1')                                            │
│  print(f"Response: {resp1.data.decode()}")                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  print("\nTesting SQL injection (id=1 OR 1=1):")                             │
│  resp2 = client.get('/user?id=1 OR 1=1')                                     │
│  print(f"Response: {resp2.data.decode()}")                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  if 'ADMIN_SECRET_123' in resp2.data.decode() and 'USER1_SECRET_456' in      │
│  resp2.data.decode():                                                        │
│      print('\n[+] SQL Injection confirmed: Both user records returned')      │
│  else:                                                                       │
│      print('\n[-] SQL Injection not confirmed')                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:10                                                     │
│    Vulnerable SQL query construction                                         │
│    return str(conn.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = %s" %             │
│  uid).fetchall())                                                            │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    - return str(conn.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = %s" %           │
│  uid).fetchall())                                                            │
│    + return str(conn.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?",             │
│  (uid,)).fetchall())                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Use parameterized queries or prepared statements instead of string          │
│  formatting. Replace the vulnerable line with: conn.execute("SELECT * FROM   │
│  users WHERE id = ?", (uid,))                                                │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0004 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Hardcoded Database Password in app.py                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/scan-canary                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A hardcoded database password was found in the source code of the           │
│  application.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Exposure of database credentials can lead to unauthorized database access   │
│  and data exfiltration.                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The application defines `DB_PASSWORD` as a static string literal. This is   │
│  a vulnerability because anyone with access to the source code (including    │
│  via other vulnerabilities like path traversal) can obtain the credential.   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Open the source file app.py.                                             │
│  2. Locate line 24.                                                          │
│  3. Observe the hardcoded password.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  with open('app.py', 'r') as f:                                              │
│      content = f.read()                                                      │
│      print(content)                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:24                                                     │
│    Remove hardcoded secret                                                   │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    - DB_PASSWORD = "SuperSecret123!"                                         │
│    + DB_PASSWORD = os.environ.get("DB_PASSWORD")                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Remove hardcoded secrets from source code and use environment variables or  │
│  a secret management system.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

